  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 AM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nNow through Saturday Night...\n\nEarly morning sat/obs shows clear to partly cloudy skies across the \nregion.  A batch of high level clouds will continue to stream across \nthe region this morning. A weak frontal boundary over southern \nIndiana will drop southeast through the region during the day.  No \nprecipitation is expected with it, but we'll see winds shift around \nto the northeast during the afternoon.  Highs today will be in the \n73-79 degree range with overnight lows dropping back into the upper \n40s/lower 50s.\n\nFor Saturday, we'll see continued low-level moisture advection ahead \nof an approaching mid-level wave and tropical moisture with the \nremnants of tropical cyclone Vance.  Our atmosphere will be quite \ndry during the day but will slowly moisten during the afternoon and \nevening.  Overall, Saturday will feature partly to mostly cloudy \nskies with highs in the lower 70s across southern Indiana and across \nnorth-central and east-central Kentucky. Slightly warmer highs will \nbe seen across southern KY with highs in the mid-upper 70s. Saturday \nevening looks to remain dry with temps mainly in the low-mid 60s.  A \nlight showers will likely start to spread into western Kentucky \novernight with lows dipping into the mid-upper 50s.\n\nSunday-Tuesday Night...\n\nSunday will feature mostly cloudy skies with scattered rain showers \nacross the region.  Rainfall could be heavy at times, especially in \nour western and southwestern zones.  Model QPF spread remains quite \nlarge with the low end of a few hundreths with the top end of the \nenvelope near an inch.  General model consensus has the highest QPF \ngenerally in the I-65 corridor and points west.  As the upper level \nwave moves east, we'll see shower activity diminish from west to \neast.  However, low clouds and sprinkles/drizzle is likely to \ncontinue into Sunday night and into Monday.  Highs on Sunday will be \nnoticeably cooler with readings in the low-mid 60s and overnight \nlows in the lower 50s.  Highs may trend a little milder on Monday \nwith highs in the mid-upper 60s.  By Tuesday, stronger southwest \nflow ahead of a stronger mid-level wave coming across the Plains \nwill help boost temps up a bit.  Mid-upper 70s look likely here, \nthough the models may under-estimate highs in this warm advection \npattern.\n\nWednesday through Friday...\n\nBy mid-week, attention will turn to a large mid-level wave heading \neastward through the central Plains.  This feature looks to arrive \nin our region by late Wednesday brining another round of moderate to \noccasionally heavy rainfall and possible thunderstorms to the \nregion.  This wave will be slow moving as it is cut off from the \nfaster flow that will be to the north, so unsettled weather is \nlikely to continue into Thursday as well.  This wave may undergo \nfurther amplification and may cut off over the Mid-Atlantic/southern \nNE US toward the end of the week.  Wrap around moisture may keep our \neastern zones a bit more cloudy even into Friday.  \n\nHighs on Wednesday will generally be in the upper 60s to the very \nlow 70s with overnight lows dropping into the upper 40s/lower 50s. \nCooler temps look likely for Thursday and Friday with highs in the \nmid-upper 60s with overnight lows in the upper 30s/lower 40s.",
